WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.297 --> 00:00:03.387
- Hi, everyone, welcome to
the Homeroom livestream.

00:00:03.387 --> 00:00:05.167
Sal here from Khan Academy.

00:00:05.167 --> 00:00:07.277
Super-excited about the conversation

00:00:07.277 --> 00:00:08.147
we're going to have today.

00:00:08.147 --> 00:00:09.137
But before we get started,

00:00:09.137 --> 00:00:12.047
I will give my standard announcements.

00:00:12.047 --> 00:00:14.767
First of all, a reminder
that we are not for profit

00:00:14.767 --> 00:00:17.317
and we can only exist through
philanthropic donations

00:00:17.317 --> 00:00:18.447
from folks like yourself.

00:00:18.447 --> 00:00:20.037
So if you're in a position to do so,

00:00:20.037 --> 00:00:23.117
please think about going
to khanacademy.org/donate

00:00:23.117 --> 00:00:24.927
and make a donation of any level.

00:00:24.927 --> 00:00:27.047
It all makes a huge difference.

00:00:27.047 --> 00:00:28.514
We've been running at a deficit pre-COVID,

00:00:28.514 --> 00:00:30.517
and that deficit has only grown

00:00:30.517 --> 00:00:32.117
as we've gone through this crisis.

00:00:32.117 --> 00:00:34.457
And with that in mind, I'd like
to give a special shout-out

00:00:34.457 --> 00:00:36.467
to several organizations
that have stepped up

00:00:36.467 --> 00:00:38.097
to help us close our gap.

00:00:38.097 --> 00:00:40.661
A special thanks to Bank
of America, google.org,

00:00:40.661 --> 00:00:43.847
AT&amp;T, Fastly, Novartis,
and the Amgen Foundation,

00:00:43.847 --> 00:00:45.717
and many others who have
supported Khan Academy

00:00:45.717 --> 00:00:47.807
through the years, to allow
us to be in a position

00:00:47.807 --> 00:00:50.167
to help folks, teachers, parents, students

00:00:50.167 --> 00:00:52.877
through the COVID crisis,
but we do need more help.

00:00:52.877 --> 00:00:55.677
So anything you can do, much appreciated.

00:00:55.677 --> 00:00:57.937
So with that, I'm super-excited

00:00:57.937 --> 00:00:59.607
to introduce our guest today.

00:00:59.607 --> 00:01:02.117
There's a lot to talk about.

00:01:02.117 --> 00:01:04.007
We have Lily Eskelsen Garcia,

00:01:04.007 --> 00:01:07.327
President of the National
Education Association,

00:01:07.327 --> 00:01:09.657
the largest teacher's union
in the country, is that right?

00:01:09.657 --> 00:01:14.610
Or one of the largest. (laughs)

00:01:19.937 --> 00:01:22.307
Thanks so much for joining us, Lily.

00:01:22.307 --> 00:01:24.220
Thanks so much for joining us, Lily.

00:01:25.070 --> 00:01:27.120
- It's an honor, thank you for having me.

00:01:28.177 --> 00:01:29.727
- So maybe a good place to start,

00:01:29.727 --> 00:01:31.917
obviously all the
conversation, and this isn't,

00:01:31.917 --> 00:01:32.917
normally this is, you know,

00:01:32.917 --> 00:01:34.467
we have these education conversations,

00:01:34.467 --> 00:01:36.327
but this has turned into
a national conversation

00:01:36.327 --> 00:01:37.867
or international conversation

00:01:37.867 --> 00:01:40.037
about the state of school closures

00:01:40.037 --> 00:01:42.817
or school openings and safety.

00:01:42.817 --> 00:01:45.947
Just where's your mind right now?

00:01:45.947 --> 00:01:47.687
Where are we in the process?

00:01:47.687 --> 00:01:49.637
How are y'all thinking about it?

00:01:49.637 --> 00:01:51.347
What are y'all advocating for?

00:01:51.347 --> 00:01:52.887
What do you see is actually happening

00:01:52.887 --> 00:01:54.077
and how is it going to play out

00:01:54.077 --> 00:01:56.427
over the next few weeks or months?

00:01:56.427 --> 00:01:58.867
- Well, Sal, it's really
why I was so excited

00:01:58.867 --> 00:02:03.017
to be on the show, because
there's so much misinformation

00:02:03.017 --> 00:02:06.457
out there about it seems like everything.

00:02:06.457 --> 00:02:10.337
So first I wanna say there is no one,

00:02:10.337 --> 00:02:13.617
probably other than their parents,

00:02:13.617 --> 00:02:16.537
who want those kids
back in-person learning

00:02:16.537 --> 00:02:20.657
more than their teachers,
and all of the counselors

00:02:20.657 --> 00:02:22.117
and the school nurse and everybody,

00:02:22.117 --> 00:02:23.807
we want our kids back.

00:02:23.807 --> 00:02:26.827
But we wanna go back
in that school building

00:02:26.827 --> 00:02:31.827
when and where we can do
it without someone dying.

00:02:33.497 --> 00:02:36.637
You know, that I even
have to say that out loud

00:02:36.637 --> 00:02:39.887
is a little weird, but I
noticed when I was subtle

00:02:39.887 --> 00:02:43.467
and I'd say without someone getting sick,

00:02:43.467 --> 00:02:46.347
bottom line is it's a
global pandemic out there.

00:02:46.347 --> 00:02:48.607
There's something in
the air swirling around

00:02:48.607 --> 00:02:49.817
that's killing people, right?

00:02:49.817 --> 00:02:51.607
Thousands and thousands of people

00:02:52.707 --> 00:02:54.657
have their lungs attacked.

00:02:54.657 --> 00:02:56.017
It's impossible for them to breathe.

00:02:56.017 --> 00:02:57.831
It's a horrible way to die.

00:02:57.831 --> 00:03:01.927
And we don't seem to all be getting that.

00:03:01.927 --> 00:03:04.827
Medical professionals have told us

00:03:04.827 --> 00:03:09.207
that the most dangerous place
to be in this global pandemic

00:03:09.207 --> 00:03:11.917
is in a closed, poorly ventilated building

00:03:11.917 --> 00:03:14.077
with lots of people crowding together,

00:03:14.077 --> 00:03:16.897
breathing on each other hour
after hour, day after day,

00:03:16.897 --> 00:03:20.117
which is the description
of my sixth grade classroom

00:03:20.117 --> 00:03:21.961
the year I had 39 kids.

00:03:21.961 --> 00:03:26.897
So I worried on any given
day that if someone coughed

00:03:26.897 --> 00:03:29.167
in the back of the room, I
was gonna catch their cold.

00:03:29.167 --> 00:03:30.857
I knew it, three times a year,

00:03:30.857 --> 00:03:32.530
I was gonna get somebody's cold.

00:03:33.387 --> 00:03:34.787
This is not that.

00:03:34.787 --> 00:03:37.587
This is quite different.

00:03:37.587 --> 00:03:40.440
This means somebody coughs
and I end up in the ICU.

00:03:41.307 --> 00:03:43.937
Somebody coughs on their best friend

00:03:43.937 --> 00:03:47.477
and this kid is infected and asymptomatic,

00:03:47.477 --> 00:03:49.917
and goes home and hugs his babysitter.

00:03:49.917 --> 00:03:50.907
Who's his babysitter?

00:03:50.907 --> 00:03:51.740
Grandma.

00:03:52.637 --> 00:03:55.440
And we know now with some,

00:03:57.957 --> 00:03:59.947
there's new information
coming out all the time,

00:03:59.947 --> 00:04:04.347
this is still so new, but
that we have people who say,

00:04:04.347 --> 00:04:06.697
kids can't get sick, kids are immune.

00:04:06.697 --> 00:04:09.857
The President, governors,
the Secretary of Education,

00:04:09.857 --> 00:04:13.280
lots of people giving
really bad information.

00:04:14.207 --> 00:04:17.187
We now know that not
only can kids get sick,

00:04:17.187 --> 00:04:19.717
not only can kids end up in the hospital,

00:04:19.717 --> 00:04:24.717
but a disproportionate amount
of those kids who are ill

00:04:25.487 --> 00:04:27.057
are minority children.

00:04:27.057 --> 00:04:28.537
They're black and brown children,

00:04:28.537 --> 00:04:30.600
they're from immigrant communities.

00:04:31.517 --> 00:04:36.327
They are at greater risk.

00:04:36.327 --> 00:04:39.967
And so they're also in the schools

00:04:39.967 --> 00:04:43.737
with the oldest buildings,
with the poorest ventilation,

00:04:43.737 --> 00:04:46.247
the most overcrowded classrooms.

00:04:46.247 --> 00:04:51.007
We have to understand that
this is dangerous to children

00:04:51.007 --> 00:04:53.347
and it is dangerous to their families,

00:04:53.347 --> 00:04:56.087
and it is dangerous to their teachers.

00:04:56.087 --> 00:05:01.067
And I am an excellent, very,
very good sixth grade teacher.

00:05:01.067 --> 00:05:03.766
You would want your kid in my sixth grade.

00:05:03.766 --> 00:05:07.487
I'm not an infectious
disease medical professional.

00:05:07.487 --> 00:05:10.647
So when I need to know
something about that,

00:05:10.647 --> 00:05:13.357
I hear what Dr. Fauci is saying

00:05:13.357 --> 00:05:15.827
or public health professionals.

00:05:15.827 --> 00:05:18.517
They're not saying anything different.

00:05:18.517 --> 00:05:21.937
They are all together,
the scientific community.

00:05:21.937 --> 00:05:23.457
The only thing we can do right now

00:05:23.457 --> 00:05:25.877
is try not to infect each other.

00:05:25.877 --> 00:05:27.967
'Cause if we do, people die.

00:05:27.967 --> 00:05:32.967
So they're saying you
don't even think about

00:05:33.577 --> 00:05:37.547
opening a school, a restaurant, a movie.

00:05:37.547 --> 00:05:40.387
You don't go to a rally

00:05:42.607 --> 00:05:46.477
until you've got this
infection rate under control.

00:05:46.477 --> 00:05:48.259
It's now skyrocketing.

00:05:48.259 --> 00:05:53.107
It's exploding in more and more places

00:05:53.107 --> 00:05:55.297
instead of fewer and fewer places.

00:05:55.297 --> 00:05:59.367
And so we're really worried
about these one-size-fits-all,

00:05:59.367 --> 00:06:01.787
open up the schools,
stuff those kids back in.

00:06:01.787 --> 00:06:02.867
Why?

00:06:02.867 --> 00:06:04.857
We need to get our jobs numbers up,

00:06:04.857 --> 00:06:08.227
we need their parents
back, it's gonna backfire.

00:06:08.227 --> 00:06:10.937
It's gonna backfire and they will be doing

00:06:10.937 --> 00:06:14.617
what they're already doing
in those premature openings.

00:06:14.617 --> 00:06:15.967
Kids are getting sick.

00:06:15.967 --> 00:06:19.382
They're infecting the
community, everybody.

00:06:19.382 --> 00:06:22.547
And now they're having
to close the schools.

00:06:22.547 --> 00:06:27.547
The chaos, the insecurity that
families are going through.

00:06:29.167 --> 00:06:32.047
It's a crisis, let's
treat it like a crisis.

00:06:32.047 --> 00:06:34.897
Listen to the people who know
what they're talking about.

00:06:35.977 --> 00:06:38.874
- You know, that makes a ton of sense.

00:06:38.874 --> 00:06:39.707
And to your point, a lot of it,

00:06:39.707 --> 00:06:42.587
it's amazing that we have to articulate it

00:06:42.587 --> 00:06:43.997
because it makes so much sense.

00:06:43.997 --> 00:06:45.777
How do you think this plays out?

00:06:45.777 --> 00:06:48.067
And then you mentioned kind
of this one-size-fits-all,

00:06:48.067 --> 00:06:51.747
everyone open schools by, no matter what,

00:06:51.747 --> 00:06:53.407
doesn't make sense,
especially where we are

00:06:53.407 --> 00:06:55.427
in the pandemic and in the incidence rate.

00:06:55.427 --> 00:06:57.637
And some of these early
cases that we're seeing,

00:06:57.637 --> 00:06:58.837
the school season hasn't even,

00:06:58.837 --> 00:07:00.427
the school year hasn't even started yet,

00:07:00.427 --> 00:07:03.607
or it's very early
stages in several places,

00:07:03.607 --> 00:07:04.837
but we're already seeing these cases.

00:07:04.837 --> 00:07:07.317
As soon as they opened up,
within three or four days,

00:07:07.317 --> 00:07:10.209
200 students, 800 students, whatever,

00:07:10.209 --> 00:07:12.150
people are testing positive.

00:07:14.167 --> 00:07:15.317
I guess there's two questions.

00:07:15.317 --> 00:07:18.797
One is, are there not
one-size-fits-all solutions

00:07:18.797 --> 00:07:21.177
that could get us a
little bit closer to this,

00:07:21.177 --> 00:07:22.692
whether it's hybrid.

00:07:22.692 --> 00:07:24.377
Could we use outdoor learning?

00:07:24.377 --> 00:07:28.017
I know during the tuberculosis
crisis last century,

00:07:28.017 --> 00:07:30.007
they did a lot of outdoor schooling.

00:07:30.007 --> 00:07:31.707
Are there things that we can do?

00:07:31.707 --> 00:07:34.857
Or are there incident rates
in certain communities

00:07:34.857 --> 00:07:37.997
at which point, the
epidemiologists are telling you

00:07:37.997 --> 00:07:39.487
that, yeah, that rate

00:07:39.487 --> 00:07:41.057
and maybe if you take
the right precautions

00:07:41.057 --> 00:07:43.967
and maybe if you use outdoor
well, it might be okay.

00:07:43.967 --> 00:07:45.837
What are you seeing there?

00:07:45.837 --> 00:07:50.137
- So I'm seeing some of
the most creative educators

00:07:50.137 --> 00:07:54.687
in the world who are really trying to say,

00:07:54.687 --> 00:07:59.127
all right, so distance,
how are we gonna distance?

00:07:59.127 --> 00:08:02.457
Disinfect, we're not talking
about a little Windex.

00:08:02.457 --> 00:08:05.157
We're talking about disinfecting

00:08:05.157 --> 00:08:07.777
using some pretty specific things.

00:08:07.777 --> 00:08:10.577
And by the way, little kids, I love 'em.

00:08:10.577 --> 00:08:12.827
I loved those 39 kids.

00:08:12.827 --> 00:08:14.797
They are little germ factories, all right?

00:08:14.797 --> 00:08:17.157
So they are touching their noses

00:08:17.157 --> 00:08:20.407
and they're touching their
little grubby mitts everywhere.

00:08:20.407 --> 00:08:24.348
So it's especially important

00:08:24.348 --> 00:08:27.487
that we have a protocol on disinfecting.

00:08:27.487 --> 00:08:29.102
Do we have the masks?

00:08:29.102 --> 00:08:34.102
And do we have the ability
to do some serious screening

00:08:35.147 --> 00:08:39.167
as kids come into the building where,

00:08:39.167 --> 00:08:40.877
do they have a cough, a temperature?

00:08:40.877 --> 00:08:42.817
Are we doing any kind of

00:08:42.817 --> 00:08:47.027
even group and random COVID testing?

00:08:47.027 --> 00:08:51.727
So all of the above are what
the disease professionals

00:08:51.727 --> 00:08:54.877
are saying we have to do, but
here's the important thing.

00:08:54.877 --> 00:08:57.447
And I've seen some of the most creative,

00:08:57.447 --> 00:08:59.897
here's how we're gonna
distance those kindergarteners.

00:08:59.897 --> 00:09:03.567
How are you gonna keep,
hula hoops, really?

00:09:03.567 --> 00:09:07.312
Yes, that's one of my members, she said,

00:09:07.312 --> 00:09:10.177
we are using creative use of space.

00:09:10.177 --> 00:09:12.247
Instead of having 20 kindergartners,

00:09:12.247 --> 00:09:14.897
we're putting like eight to 10 in a room.

00:09:14.897 --> 00:09:16.737
We took out all the desks

00:09:16.737 --> 00:09:20.547
and we put hula hoops
and pillows on the floor,

00:09:20.547 --> 00:09:21.967
and that's their space.

00:09:21.967 --> 00:09:25.277
And they don't go out of
their hula hoop space.

00:09:25.277 --> 00:09:26.587
They're not gonna wear the mask.

00:09:26.587 --> 00:09:28.637
Little kids, that's a toy.

00:09:28.637 --> 00:09:31.127
So they're wearing space helmets.

00:09:31.127 --> 00:09:32.297
They're going to the moon.

00:09:32.297 --> 00:09:34.417
And they've got the plexiglass helmets.

00:09:34.417 --> 00:09:36.937
They're doing what they
can with what they have,

00:09:36.937 --> 00:09:38.497
which isn't a lot.

00:09:38.497 --> 00:09:40.530
But here's what I want people to know.

00:09:41.677 --> 00:09:44.400
Folks keep forgetting
the most important part.

00:09:45.677 --> 00:09:50.447
The number of the infection
rate in your community

00:09:50.447 --> 00:09:53.127
is going to be the infection rate

00:09:53.127 --> 00:09:55.207
of the people walking into that building.

00:09:55.207 --> 00:09:58.147
The teacher lives in the neighborhood,

00:09:58.147 --> 00:10:01.987
those kids and their families
live in those neighborhoods.

00:10:01.987 --> 00:10:04.627
That school is a reflection

00:10:04.627 --> 00:10:07.177
of what's going on in
the broader community.

00:10:07.177 --> 00:10:11.557
And so if you have, like
they had in New Orleans,

00:10:11.557 --> 00:10:16.157
a 20 or 25% infection rate,

00:10:16.157 --> 00:10:20.247
you can probably bet that 25%
of the big and little people

00:10:20.247 --> 00:10:22.257
that walk into that school

00:10:22.257 --> 00:10:24.257
are going to be infected by COVID.

00:10:24.257 --> 00:10:27.527
Only this time you're putting
them in these classrooms

00:10:27.527 --> 00:10:31.977
that have poor ventilation
that, even with your hula hoops,

00:10:31.977 --> 00:10:34.410
they're gonna be passing
each other in the hall.

00:10:35.277 --> 00:10:37.387
That's why the first thing,

00:10:37.387 --> 00:10:38.877
and it's the thing most people say,

00:10:38.877 --> 00:10:40.997
yeah, well, we don't
really care about that.

00:10:40.997 --> 00:10:44.077
Care about the community infection rate

00:10:44.077 --> 00:10:49.077
until you get it down to about
5% where it's manageable,

00:10:50.377 --> 00:10:53.107
where you can say maybe 5% of the folks

00:10:53.107 --> 00:10:56.447
coming into this school,
and we can screen for them.

00:10:56.447 --> 00:11:00.407
We can screen and do some
health checks for them.

00:11:00.407 --> 00:11:02.057
But that's the most important part

00:11:02.057 --> 00:11:04.827
and it's the part that's out of our hands.

00:11:04.827 --> 00:11:08.077
All the hula hoops and
face masks in the world

00:11:08.077 --> 00:11:12.607
will not help a surging virus rate.

00:11:12.607 --> 00:11:14.417
And so that's what we're worried about,

00:11:14.417 --> 00:11:16.697
it's that folks are looking at this list,

00:11:16.697 --> 00:11:21.007
infection rates, masks,
disinfectant, distancing.

00:11:21.007 --> 00:11:23.737
And there's people who don't
really know what they're doing

00:11:23.737 --> 00:11:28.127
saying, well, let's do the
masks and the plexiglass,

00:11:28.127 --> 00:11:30.847
but we don't know how
to distance the kids.

00:11:30.847 --> 00:11:33.647
And yeah, we've got a 3% infection rate,

00:11:33.647 --> 00:11:34.737
we can't do anything about it.

00:11:34.737 --> 00:11:38.767
So let's just pick and
choose what might be easiest,

00:11:38.767 --> 00:11:41.067
most convenient, least expensive.

00:11:41.067 --> 00:11:42.527
It doesn't work that way.

00:11:42.527 --> 00:11:45.487
This is complicated, it's hard.

00:11:45.487 --> 00:11:48.907
And it's expensive if
you're gonna do it right.

00:11:48.907 --> 00:11:52.367
I was alarmed when I
heard the President say,

00:11:52.367 --> 00:11:54.747
well, I looked at the guidance,
that costs a lot of money,

00:11:54.747 --> 00:11:57.490
and I told 'em to go back and rewrite it.

00:11:58.624 --> 00:12:00.847
That's not how it works, don't do that.

00:12:00.847 --> 00:12:03.920
Listen to the people who
are trying to save our lives

00:12:03.920 --> 00:12:07.037
and why wouldn't we listen to them?

00:12:07.037 --> 00:12:11.437
So, number one, I'm a little
mad, okay, I'm very angry

00:12:11.437 --> 00:12:14.717
at a lot of politicians,
local, state, and national,

00:12:14.717 --> 00:12:16.817
who told folks, we don't
have to have a plan.

00:12:16.817 --> 00:12:19.207
It'll go away, it's magic.

00:12:19.207 --> 00:12:22.037
Nope, it's science, it's really science.

00:12:22.037 --> 00:12:27.037
And what they didn't do
in January and February

00:12:28.577 --> 00:12:31.900
is the result, is that
we've prolonged this.

00:12:32.833 --> 00:12:37.130
We are the outlier in the
United States of America.

00:12:38.117 --> 00:12:43.117
There are countries who
do not have infection,

00:12:46.157 --> 00:12:47.550
got it very early.

00:12:49.850 --> 00:12:54.167
And now I'm getting in questions like,

00:12:54.167 --> 00:12:56.787
what are you going to do, teachers,

00:12:56.787 --> 00:13:01.350
to make sure we can still keep them safe?

00:13:02.527 --> 00:13:04.607
What's your plan B?
- Yeah.

00:13:04.607 --> 00:13:05.640
- I don't have one.

00:13:07.197 --> 00:13:08.697
- And what you're describing is complete--

00:13:08.697 --> 00:13:10.877
- And it's not a fair
question to ask a teacher,

00:13:10.877 --> 00:13:13.010
what about the politicians?

00:13:14.386 --> 00:13:16.107
- And what you're describing

00:13:16.107 --> 00:13:18.027
makes all the sense in the world.

00:13:18.027 --> 00:13:20.527
You know, we had Randi
Weingarten, your peer,

00:13:20.527 --> 00:13:22.267
at the National Federation of Teachers

00:13:22.267 --> 00:13:23.567
saying essentially the same things.

00:13:23.567 --> 00:13:25.837
You know, you have to
take basic precautions,

00:13:25.837 --> 00:13:28.647
do contact tracing, and
then get your infection rate

00:13:28.647 --> 00:13:31.817
in a community down to a certain
threshold, at which point,

00:13:31.817 --> 00:13:33.797
your point you're making
and Randi Weingarten made

00:13:33.797 --> 00:13:35.217
is that like, it's not
that people are saying

00:13:35.217 --> 00:13:36.517
it has to be risk-free.

00:13:36.517 --> 00:13:39.017
Obviously, even in pre-COVID,
there's all sorts of risks

00:13:39.017 --> 00:13:39.850
in our life.

00:13:39.850 --> 00:13:41.937
But it just has to
become a reasonable risk.

00:13:41.937 --> 00:13:45.287
And it's not just about
teachers or students.

00:13:45.287 --> 00:13:46.547
This is about the community.

00:13:46.547 --> 00:13:49.617
This is just epidemiologically sound,

00:13:49.617 --> 00:13:53.011
I have trouble saying that
word, for the entire community.

00:13:53.011 --> 00:13:57.293
So we actually had Dr.
Fauci on the same livestream

00:13:57.293 --> 00:13:59.797
and the best that I could understand

00:13:59.797 --> 00:14:01.027
when I asked him the questions

00:14:01.027 --> 00:14:02.797
of how this is all going to play out,

00:14:02.797 --> 00:14:05.937
it really feels like the
most optimistic scenario

00:14:05.937 --> 00:14:09.397
is one of these vaccines that
are currently in phase three,

00:14:09.397 --> 00:14:10.877
which we've already started manufacturing

00:14:10.877 --> 00:14:14.862
essentially speculatively,
that they might, hopefully,

00:14:14.862 --> 00:14:18.587
prove effective in the
December/January timeframe.

00:14:18.587 --> 00:14:20.827
At which point we have this
massive logistical challenge

00:14:20.827 --> 00:14:23.385
of distributing hundreds of
millions of doses of vaccine.

00:14:23.385 --> 00:14:25.617
And we don't know to
what degree they'll work

00:14:25.617 --> 00:14:26.847
or how many people take it.

00:14:26.847 --> 00:14:29.162
Simultaneously, you have
the therapeutic lens where,

00:14:29.162 --> 00:14:31.867
hopefully, some therapies will come out

00:14:31.867 --> 00:14:34.077
in the January/February timeframe

00:14:34.077 --> 00:14:37.557
where COVID can be less
deadly, less severe.

00:14:37.557 --> 00:14:39.477
Hopefully, we get to some of those things.

00:14:39.477 --> 00:14:41.267
But any way you look at it,
everyone I've talked to,

00:14:41.267 --> 00:14:45.407
we had the CEO of Novartis
on this livestream as well.

00:14:45.407 --> 00:14:47.377
They're all saying it's kind of a year

00:14:47.377 --> 00:14:50.917
that we're in this world
that you've just described.

00:14:50.917 --> 00:14:53.817
And so how are you thinking about it?

00:14:53.817 --> 00:14:56.747
How do you think educators
should think about

00:14:56.747 --> 00:15:00.787
what looks like it could be
at least a full school year

00:15:00.787 --> 00:15:02.710
of some form of distance learning?

00:15:05.517 --> 00:15:07.987
- First of all, we were so thrilled

00:15:07.987 --> 00:15:10.647
that the House of Representatives,

00:15:10.647 --> 00:15:12.497
and who says they're ever
thrilled with anything

00:15:12.497 --> 00:15:15.857
that Congress does, but
we were actually thrilled

00:15:15.857 --> 00:15:16.977
that they listened to us.

00:15:16.977 --> 00:15:21.474
And they said, yes, you
prepare for the face masks

00:15:21.474 --> 00:15:24.597
and the disinfectant, and yes,
we need all of these things.

00:15:24.597 --> 00:15:26.717
And please don't make us have to lay off

00:15:26.717 --> 00:15:28.077
the school nurse, all right?

00:15:28.077 --> 00:15:33.077
So we got a chunk of money from
the House of Representatives

00:15:33.127 --> 00:15:35.927
that is languishing now
with Mitch McConnell

00:15:35.927 --> 00:15:37.907
not wanting to bring it to a vote.

00:15:37.907 --> 00:15:40.677
But one of the things we said was,

00:15:40.677 --> 00:15:43.937
and you do have to have simultaneously

00:15:43.937 --> 00:15:45.777
the ability for a school district

00:15:45.777 --> 00:15:48.127
to deliver distance learning.

00:15:48.127 --> 00:15:51.597
And so we asked for all kinds of funding

00:15:51.597 --> 00:15:56.597
for a family to be able to
bring Wi-Fi into their home.

00:15:56.987 --> 00:15:59.760
That's like indoor plumbing now, you know?

00:16:02.407 --> 00:16:06.527
And guess what, not
everybody's mom has a laptop

00:16:06.527 --> 00:16:10.957
and has an extra tablet
sitting around the house.

00:16:10.957 --> 00:16:14.377
We kinda take that for granted
that, well, everyone has.

00:16:14.377 --> 00:16:17.827
No, stop thinking everyone
has just 'cause you have it.

00:16:17.827 --> 00:16:22.727
And so there's some
extra money, $4 billion

00:16:22.727 --> 00:16:24.317
that would help with the technology

00:16:24.317 --> 00:16:28.037
that could deliver distance learning,

00:16:28.037 --> 00:16:31.907
especially to the neediest families

00:16:31.907 --> 00:16:34.707
who are least likely to
be able to afford things

00:16:34.707 --> 00:16:38.307
like an extra tablet
and Wi-Fi in their home.

00:16:38.307 --> 00:16:41.987
When we ran out of those school buildings,

00:16:41.987 --> 00:16:46.537
120,000 school buildings back in March,

00:16:46.537 --> 00:16:48.757
like someone pulled the fire alarm

00:16:48.757 --> 00:16:53.757
and all simultaneously 120,000
school buildings emptied out,

00:16:55.897 --> 00:16:58.027
it wasn't for the fun of it.

00:16:58.027 --> 00:17:03.027
It was because infectious
disease, medical experts said

00:17:04.267 --> 00:17:06.237
this is a pandemic.

00:17:06.237 --> 00:17:07.447
It is growing.

00:17:07.447 --> 00:17:10.107
We have to do something to
stop infecting each other.

00:17:10.107 --> 00:17:13.047
And a school is like the worst place

00:17:13.047 --> 00:17:14.357
to do something like that.

00:17:14.357 --> 00:17:15.690
So we left.

00:17:16.787 --> 00:17:21.787
And within a day, we had
people who were not trained

00:17:22.947 --> 00:17:26.727
in delivering virtual
classes saying, all right,

00:17:26.727 --> 00:17:27.947
what's this thing called Zoom?

00:17:27.947 --> 00:17:29.097
What are we doin' here?

00:17:29.977 --> 00:17:32.077
And they were going online

00:17:32.077 --> 00:17:33.407
and they were getting information,

00:17:33.407 --> 00:17:35.827
and they were connecting with each other.

00:17:35.827 --> 00:17:39.717
My organization, we were just
bombarded with people saying,

00:17:39.717 --> 00:17:42.747
what you got, I gotta do this tomorrow.

00:17:42.747 --> 00:17:46.707
And I've got third graders,
or I've got a calculus class,

00:17:46.707 --> 00:17:49.957
or I've got special ed students,

00:17:49.957 --> 00:17:53.157
or I've got kids that don't speak English,

00:17:53.157 --> 00:17:57.187
and I've got to deliver
instruction and sometimes services,

00:17:57.187 --> 00:18:00.367
and sometimes lunch to these kids.

00:18:00.367 --> 00:18:03.177
'Cause that's what a school
is, it's a community.

00:18:03.177 --> 00:18:07.207
And it does take a village to
deliver what those kids need.

00:18:07.207 --> 00:18:08.537
Sometimes that school nurse

00:18:08.537 --> 00:18:11.697
is that family's primary care physician,

00:18:11.697 --> 00:18:15.467
because they can't afford
to go to the doctor.

00:18:15.467 --> 00:18:19.557
So we took all of that,
we pulled it together.

00:18:19.557 --> 00:18:21.017
Oh, my Lord, it wasn't perfect.

00:18:21.017 --> 00:18:23.330
It was frustrating,
it's still frustrating.

00:18:24.357 --> 00:18:29.357
But we knew in May, people were saying,

00:18:29.877 --> 00:18:32.117
this is going in the wrong direction.

00:18:32.117 --> 00:18:34.857
This is not gonna be,
okay, now they told us

00:18:34.857 --> 00:18:37.097
the warm weather would clear it all.

00:18:37.097 --> 00:18:39.007
Nope, didn't clear it all up.

00:18:39.007 --> 00:18:43.237
And in fact, they opened up some places

00:18:44.677 --> 00:18:46.377
that they shouldn't have.

00:18:46.377 --> 00:18:48.867
And folks rushed into the bar

00:18:48.867 --> 00:18:51.027
and they should've known better.

00:18:51.027 --> 00:18:55.407
And they ended up getting
sick, getting other people sick

00:18:55.407 --> 00:18:59.407
who went to work, who sat on
a bus, who did what people do.

00:18:59.407 --> 00:19:03.377
And all of a sudden things are
spiking all over the country.

00:19:03.377 --> 00:19:07.507
So it became very, very clear to teachers,

00:19:07.507 --> 00:19:11.369
to school boards and even to governors,

00:19:11.369 --> 00:19:13.237
okay, this is not going away.

00:19:13.237 --> 00:19:15.507
What are we gonna do
in August and September

00:19:15.507 --> 00:19:18.067
as schools start to reopen?

00:19:18.067 --> 00:19:20.517
And that's when we, it was in May,

00:19:20.517 --> 00:19:21.687
we went to the House and said,

00:19:21.687 --> 00:19:23.807
you've gotta give us some
money for technology,

00:19:23.807 --> 00:19:27.717
for home technology, for
kids that don't have access,

00:19:27.717 --> 00:19:29.297
don't have opportunity.

00:19:29.297 --> 00:19:31.065
We were delivering to some of these kids

00:19:31.065 --> 00:19:33.347
paper packets of worksheets.

00:19:33.347 --> 00:19:36.430
I hate worksheets, but
it was all we could do.

00:19:37.378 --> 00:19:41.747
The only technology in some
homes was mom's telephone

00:19:41.747 --> 00:19:43.477
and she took it to work with her

00:19:43.477 --> 00:19:45.813
'cause she had a service job

00:19:45.813 --> 00:19:48.677
stacking grocery shelves or something.

00:19:48.677 --> 00:19:53.327
And we couldn't even call on the telephone

00:19:53.327 --> 00:19:55.567
our students and find
out how they were doing

00:19:55.567 --> 00:19:58.287
or give them some instruction.

00:19:58.287 --> 00:20:02.787
And we're still just appalled that in May,

00:20:02.787 --> 00:20:06.047
we thought we were on a
fast track to getting this,

00:20:06.047 --> 00:20:08.927
and it's still sitting in the Senate.

00:20:08.927 --> 00:20:13.927
So abandon all hope something
coming from the Senate

00:20:14.157 --> 00:20:16.357
that's gonna get to these kids in time.

00:20:16.357 --> 00:20:20.957
Schools are already opening
and they didn't do it right.

00:20:20.957 --> 00:20:24.537
And those schools, many of
them, are already closing.

00:20:24.537 --> 00:20:27.387
And it's causing chaos in families

00:20:27.387 --> 00:20:30.117
when we could have had
a plan, we could have,

00:20:30.117 --> 00:20:32.484
it didn't have to be like this.

00:20:32.484 --> 00:20:36.467
But now we're still trying to figure out.

00:20:36.467 --> 00:20:40.777
Now in some places, in Nebraska,
one of my colleagues called

00:20:40.777 --> 00:20:44.977
and said, we know which of
our kids didn't have Wi-Fi.

00:20:44.977 --> 00:20:46.350
We just lost them.

00:20:47.187 --> 00:20:50.327
So now we're working with
local television stations.

00:20:50.327 --> 00:20:55.069
We're just talking about
you've got a $10 antenna

00:20:55.069 --> 00:20:58.530
on your old-fashioned
TV, it's not even cable.

00:20:59.387 --> 00:21:04.007
But some of the TV stations
are giving us an hour

00:21:04.007 --> 00:21:06.827
where we could say, this
is for younger grades,

00:21:06.827 --> 00:21:08.854
this is for middle school,
this is for high school.

00:21:08.854 --> 00:21:12.047
And we're doing some general
project-based learning

00:21:12.047 --> 00:21:14.497
so at least there's something.

00:21:14.497 --> 00:21:15.584
At least there's something,

00:21:15.584 --> 00:21:20.584
and the parents can tune
into a television program.

00:21:21.757 --> 00:21:23.427
We're doing whatever it takes.

00:21:23.427 --> 00:21:26.507
We wish that some of the lawmakers,

00:21:26.507 --> 00:21:29.357
the people that have the purse strings,

00:21:29.357 --> 00:21:32.437
we wish that they would step up

00:21:32.437 --> 00:21:35.387
and see that they could be helping us

00:21:35.387 --> 00:21:37.697
instead of being the obstacle

00:21:37.697 --> 00:21:40.570
for getting something very important done.

00:21:41.857 --> 00:21:43.667
- And what would you think
would be kind of the order,

00:21:43.667 --> 00:21:45.580
let's hope, and I agree with you 100%,

00:21:45.580 --> 00:21:47.587
in a world where we're
doing a trillion here

00:21:47.587 --> 00:21:50.507
and a trillion there of stimulus packages,

00:21:50.507 --> 00:21:54.117
the Internet connectivity issue
is a small pittance there.

00:21:54.117 --> 00:21:57.567
But let's hope that we're
able to get the devices

00:21:57.567 --> 00:21:59.897
and get the Internet access
for a lot of these students.

00:21:59.897 --> 00:22:01.177
And I know that a lot of school districts

00:22:01.177 --> 00:22:02.357
have been doing some heroic things,

00:22:02.357 --> 00:22:05.597
distributing tens or hundreds
of thousands of devices.

00:22:05.597 --> 00:22:08.377
Even then we've been hearing about

00:22:08.377 --> 00:22:09.487
there's some subset of kids

00:22:09.487 --> 00:22:11.487
that just don't have
enough supports at home,

00:22:11.487 --> 00:22:14.317
maybe not ideal environments,
you know, 5%, 10% of kids

00:22:14.317 --> 00:22:16.047
that even if they're getting the access,

00:22:16.047 --> 00:22:17.497
they're just getting disengaged.

00:22:17.497 --> 00:22:20.557
So if we had more resources,
where would it go?

00:22:20.557 --> 00:22:22.677
Would it go towards, you
know, there's a question here

00:22:22.677 --> 00:22:25.209
from Janet Martin Rodriguez on Facebook,

00:22:25.209 --> 00:22:27.247
are teachers getting
enough support and training

00:22:27.247 --> 00:22:29.347
they need to keep students
engaged, even excited?

00:22:29.347 --> 00:22:31.827
So teacher training is one element.

00:22:31.827 --> 00:22:35.557
There is, more social
supports is another element.

00:22:35.557 --> 00:22:38.400
I don't know how you deliver
that in a world of COVID.

00:22:39.327 --> 00:22:41.867
Then there's obviously
online tools and instruction,

00:22:41.867 --> 00:22:45.067
obviously what we do at Khan
Academy, and other tools.

00:22:45.067 --> 00:22:48.097
Where do you see, you know,
if you had a blank check

00:22:48.097 --> 00:22:49.947
and I know that's a little
bit of daydreaming right now,

00:22:49.947 --> 00:22:50.967
but I would love, you know,

00:22:50.967 --> 00:22:53.067
I think there might be
an openness, hopefully,

00:22:53.067 --> 00:22:55.254
at some point when people
realize how serious this is,

00:22:55.254 --> 00:22:59.117
what would be the order of operations?

00:22:59.117 --> 00:23:01.707
- And I'm never going to
be one of the Pollyanna,

00:23:01.707 --> 00:23:04.137
let's make lemonade out of this.

00:23:04.137 --> 00:23:05.977
This is horrible and people are dying.

00:23:05.977 --> 00:23:08.827
And I'm not gonna say,
oh, but the good news.

00:23:08.827 --> 00:23:11.597
There's no, there's no good news here.

00:23:11.597 --> 00:23:16.597
But let me tell you the
urgency that it has produced

00:23:18.037 --> 00:23:22.957
is how important that
basic teacher training.

00:23:22.957 --> 00:23:26.377
And I know I'm talking about,
you said like a magic wand

00:23:26.377 --> 00:23:30.017
or a blank check, and I'm
saying what if we could go

00:23:30.017 --> 00:23:33.457
in a time machine to the future,

00:23:33.457 --> 00:23:37.187
I know that this is the long
game, this is the long-term.

00:23:37.187 --> 00:23:42.187
But we need it, we need how to engage

00:23:43.307 --> 00:23:47.877
with teaching on technology,
distance learning,

00:23:47.877 --> 00:23:49.917
what that looks like if
you're an art teacher,

00:23:49.917 --> 00:23:51.407
if you're a calculus teacher,

00:23:51.407 --> 00:23:53.617
if you're a third grade teacher.

00:23:53.617 --> 00:23:56.017
I'm a sixth grade teacher

00:23:56.017 --> 00:24:00.727
and I know that you will
be impressed to learn

00:24:00.727 --> 00:24:03.867
that I have a Master's degree
in instructional technology,

00:24:03.867 --> 00:24:07.767
which I got on an Atari 800 in 1983.

00:24:07.767 --> 00:24:12.767
So yeah, I need to like up
my game just a tiny bit.

00:24:13.847 --> 00:24:16.427
I can do Oregon Trail
like nobody's business.

00:24:16.427 --> 00:24:20.996
But I'm thinking that maybe,
maybe I could upgrade.

00:24:20.996 --> 00:24:25.996
Most of us, unless you
were in a special program

00:24:26.827 --> 00:24:30.757
that did distance learning
for kids in rural areas,

00:24:30.757 --> 00:24:33.607
we do have some educators

00:24:33.607 --> 00:24:37.717
who their specialty is distance learning.

00:24:37.717 --> 00:24:40.387
Now we have to look at this like

00:24:40.387 --> 00:24:43.837
technology is your blackboard,

00:24:43.837 --> 00:24:48.293
technology is the chalk you use.

00:24:48.293 --> 00:24:50.777
It's how you have to deliver.

00:24:50.777 --> 00:24:53.937
This isn't gonna be the
first time and the last time

00:24:53.937 --> 00:24:56.347
that we have some kind of emergency

00:24:56.347 --> 00:25:01.117
where kids, where they can't
walk into that school building.

00:25:01.117 --> 00:25:03.957
We've done things with snow days

00:25:03.957 --> 00:25:06.357
and there's a hurricane or a tornado.

00:25:06.357 --> 00:25:09.877
I mean, we've had these horrible crises

00:25:09.877 --> 00:25:11.967
where the school got blown away.

00:25:11.967 --> 00:25:15.797
Look at how long Katrina
took to recover from.

00:25:15.797 --> 00:25:19.677
And there was no plan B for these kids.

00:25:19.677 --> 00:25:23.857
So now maybe our eyes are open

00:25:23.857 --> 00:25:27.707
that becoming a teacher means
you know about your subject,

00:25:27.707 --> 00:25:30.037
you know how to teach that subject,

00:25:30.037 --> 00:25:33.127
and those are two different
things, by the way,

00:25:33.127 --> 00:25:38.127
and you know all the tools
that you may have to use

00:25:38.937 --> 00:25:40.227
to teach that subject.

00:25:40.227 --> 00:25:44.827
And technology is going to
be more and more important

00:25:44.827 --> 00:25:47.737
that we become comfortable with it.

00:25:47.737 --> 00:25:50.807
And that we understand how important it is

00:25:50.807 --> 00:25:54.707
to have that technology in a child's home

00:25:54.707 --> 00:25:57.227
so that they can do their homework,

00:25:57.227 --> 00:26:01.497
not just in case of a global pandemic.

00:26:01.497 --> 00:26:05.377
But we've always known
that the homework gap

00:26:06.637 --> 00:26:11.637
mimics the technology gap
in that child's living space

00:26:12.237 --> 00:26:14.297
in that child's home.

00:26:14.297 --> 00:26:19.237
And that there are kids
who have wonderful tools,

00:26:19.237 --> 00:26:20.417
they have access.

00:26:20.417 --> 00:26:23.247
I wouldn't take anything
away from anybody.

00:26:23.247 --> 00:26:25.507
But it was important for those kids,

00:26:25.507 --> 00:26:28.707
and their parents were able to afford it

00:26:28.707 --> 00:26:31.157
and saying, yep, this is
really, really important

00:26:31.157 --> 00:26:32.727
for my kid to succeed.

00:26:32.727 --> 00:26:33.727
They're right.

00:26:33.727 --> 00:26:36.647
It's important for all
kids to have that access

00:26:36.647 --> 00:26:38.227
and that opportunity.

00:26:38.227 --> 00:26:41.267
And just like we would
say in the school building

00:26:41.267 --> 00:26:44.287
for those kids, we
fight for a kid's access

00:26:44.287 --> 00:26:49.287
to a school nurse, a librarian,
a counselor, the arts,

00:26:50.037 --> 00:26:54.466
the broad range of programs
that every child needs.

00:26:54.466 --> 00:26:58.437
And now they're all gonna need access

00:26:58.437 --> 00:27:03.117
not just to the equipment,
that's actually the easy part,

00:27:03.117 --> 00:27:05.547
but access to a professional

00:27:05.547 --> 00:27:09.517
who knows what she or he is doing

00:27:09.517 --> 00:27:13.087
to make sure that those kids

00:27:13.087 --> 00:27:16.837
who are learning that way
are being taught well.

00:27:16.837 --> 00:27:18.867
It's not my favorite way to teach.

00:27:18.867 --> 00:27:19.767
It really isn't.

00:27:19.767 --> 00:27:24.427
I love to hug kids, I can't do that now.

00:27:24.427 --> 00:27:27.537
And I don't want them to fall behind.

00:27:27.537 --> 00:27:29.477
And I want them to be safe.

00:27:29.477 --> 00:27:32.417
So if I'm a true
professional, I have to say,

00:27:32.417 --> 00:27:34.217
all right, on a dime,

00:27:34.217 --> 00:27:37.757
I know how to best use this technology.

00:27:37.757 --> 00:27:42.757
I know the do's and don'ts,
and I know what my audience,

00:27:43.137 --> 00:27:46.767
what my students need from me.

00:27:46.767 --> 00:27:48.697
And sometimes they need a hug

00:27:48.697 --> 00:27:51.759
and sometimes they need me
to know how to engage them

00:27:51.759 --> 00:27:55.327
on a screen that they're looking at.

00:27:55.327 --> 00:27:56.350
I want those skills.

00:27:57.360 --> 00:27:58.577
- And what do you think
that could look like?

00:27:58.577 --> 00:28:02.767
Obviously, we can't
paint the full picture,

00:28:02.767 --> 00:28:03.857
but sketch what it looks like.

00:28:03.857 --> 00:28:07.048
'Cause I think to your point,
everyone's been talking about

00:28:07.048 --> 00:28:10.477
just the social distancing,
and do we open or close.

00:28:10.477 --> 00:28:11.357
And then the next level

00:28:11.357 --> 00:28:13.927
they've been talking about device access.

00:28:13.927 --> 00:28:15.367
But there hasn't been
a lot of conversation

00:28:15.367 --> 00:28:17.847
about just what does at least adequate,

00:28:17.847 --> 00:28:18.997
we don't even have to talk about great,

00:28:18.997 --> 00:28:21.145
but what does an adequate
experience look like

00:28:21.145 --> 00:28:22.507
in this world?

00:28:22.507 --> 00:28:25.447
If you could sketch, what
would be your expectations

00:28:25.447 --> 00:28:27.857
for your own children or if
you were in the classroom

00:28:27.857 --> 00:28:29.117
teaching a sixth grade classroom,

00:28:29.117 --> 00:28:32.877
how would you run your
classroom in math or English

00:28:32.877 --> 00:28:35.097
or social sciences right now?

00:28:35.097 --> 00:28:37.887
- Well, one of the things that
we know is a great advantage

00:28:37.887 --> 00:28:40.887
to our students is to have mom or dad

00:28:40.887 --> 00:28:44.527
or a big brother or sister helping them,

00:28:44.527 --> 00:28:46.317
especially if you're
dealing with younger kids.

00:28:46.317 --> 00:28:49.167
But I think it's the
same with older kids too,

00:28:49.167 --> 00:28:54.167
where they don't plug
in or they don't engage.

00:28:54.837 --> 00:28:59.837
And part of it may be that we
need to know how to develop

00:29:01.607 --> 00:29:05.057
better pacing and better lesson plans,

00:29:05.057 --> 00:29:09.707
and things that are meant to
be engaging on that screen.

00:29:09.707 --> 00:29:13.437
Right now for a lot of our
teachers, it was all right,

00:29:13.437 --> 00:29:17.467
we've got a free Zoom or
we've got a free something,

00:29:17.467 --> 00:29:19.537
and now go do whatever you were doing

00:29:19.537 --> 00:29:24.537
with your 35 kids yesterday,
and now just do the same thing

00:29:25.037 --> 00:29:28.177
looking at them sitting
in 35 different kitchens

00:29:28.177 --> 00:29:30.567
on their tablets and laptops.

00:29:30.567 --> 00:29:34.747
And we know that that's
not the best pedagogy.

00:29:34.747 --> 00:29:36.787
We know that you have to engage kids

00:29:36.787 --> 00:29:38.607
and you have to ask them questions,

00:29:38.607 --> 00:29:41.337
and they have to be able to ask questions

00:29:41.337 --> 00:29:43.487
and interact with each other.

00:29:43.487 --> 00:29:46.526
And there's ways that we can do that.

00:29:46.526 --> 00:29:51.047
NEA is actually giving
some webinars for teachers

00:29:51.047 --> 00:29:53.347
that have never taught
online that are saying,

00:29:53.347 --> 00:29:56.027
I just need a, I need a primer on this.

00:29:56.027 --> 00:30:01.027
I don't know the first thing
about what this format can do,

00:30:01.217 --> 00:30:05.067
or my district is using
this kind of a platform,

00:30:05.067 --> 00:30:07.407
and I don't know what a platform is.

00:30:07.407 --> 00:30:08.877
So, I mean, we're talking about

00:30:08.877 --> 00:30:13.667
strict, strict, strict beginners
and doing some webinars.

00:30:13.667 --> 00:30:17.047
Anybody that wants to find out more about

00:30:17.047 --> 00:30:19.677
what NEA is just putting out there free,

00:30:19.677 --> 00:30:24.187
it's nea.org/covidaction.

00:30:24.187 --> 00:30:27.737
Because these are the actions
that many of our members,

00:30:27.737 --> 00:30:29.207
thousands and thousands have said,

00:30:29.207 --> 00:30:30.637
if you can do anything for me,

00:30:30.637 --> 00:30:34.497
just help me know what the options are.

00:30:34.497 --> 00:30:37.717
Because I don't wanna
just look at a screen

00:30:37.717 --> 00:30:41.627
and recite from a textbook.

00:30:41.627 --> 00:30:44.876
I know what I'm doing
when I'm with those kids.

00:30:44.876 --> 00:30:49.876
How do I get to something
that looks like that online?

00:30:51.264 --> 00:30:55.827
And so, again, I'm not an expert in that,

00:30:55.827 --> 00:30:58.117
but we're putting some of the teachers

00:30:58.117 --> 00:31:00.957
that have done this before.

00:31:00.957 --> 00:31:03.707
And we wanna look at
what you're doing, Sal,

00:31:03.707 --> 00:31:07.757
with your online experience.

00:31:07.757 --> 00:31:12.757
Because people are desperate to say,

00:31:13.047 --> 00:31:16.767
if I don't know what I'm supposed
to know, I gotta learn it

00:31:16.767 --> 00:31:20.447
and I got a short time
to really ramp this up.

00:31:20.447 --> 00:31:25.447
Because we don't want to
close teaching and learning.

00:31:26.417 --> 00:31:29.417
I've had too many reporters who've said,

00:31:29.417 --> 00:31:32.797
are you for closing the
schools, are you going to?

00:31:32.797 --> 00:31:35.827
And the whole thing, it's
like, this is the building.

00:31:35.827 --> 00:31:39.577
And yeah, we don't want
the building to be unsafe,

00:31:39.577 --> 00:31:42.417
unhealthy, we don't
wanna rush into a place

00:31:42.417 --> 00:31:45.837
that they told us to rush out
of because it wasn't safe.

00:31:45.837 --> 00:31:47.601
So let's get that part right.

00:31:47.601 --> 00:31:50.997
But we don't wanna close
teaching and learning.

00:31:50.997 --> 00:31:52.417
Those kids need us.

00:31:52.417 --> 00:31:54.377
They need us to deliver instruction,

00:31:54.377 --> 00:31:56.387
they need us to deliver services.

00:31:56.387 --> 00:31:58.637
They need us to deliver lunch.

00:31:58.637 --> 00:32:03.007
So we're trying to develop our own plan

00:32:03.007 --> 00:32:06.127
as a learning community,
sometimes school by school

00:32:06.127 --> 00:32:07.640
or district by district.

00:32:08.737 --> 00:32:12.377
And along with all of
those practical things,

00:32:12.377 --> 00:32:17.377
I wanna know how to use
this so that my kids

00:32:17.827 --> 00:32:20.147
get all I can give them.

00:32:20.147 --> 00:32:24.537
And for us it's not like,
another reporter said,

00:32:24.537 --> 00:32:28.847
are you scared that now that
everyone knows how easy it is

00:32:28.847 --> 00:32:32.594
to teach online and that parents are just,

00:32:32.594 --> 00:32:37.594
they know how easy it is to
just turn on their computer?

00:32:37.647 --> 00:32:40.717
And I went, you know, talk
to some of those parents.

00:32:40.717 --> 00:32:42.487
'Cause they're the ones
that are begging the schools

00:32:42.487 --> 00:32:43.570
to please open up.

00:32:44.527 --> 00:32:45.757
It's not easy.

00:32:45.757 --> 00:32:47.627
It's hard to be a teacher.

00:32:47.627 --> 00:32:50.127
It's hard to do this right.

00:32:50.127 --> 00:32:51.257
We love our work.

00:32:51.257 --> 00:32:53.157
We're professionals at it.

00:32:53.157 --> 00:32:57.237
We put a lot of sweat equity into it.

00:32:57.237 --> 00:32:59.327
It's a labor of love for us.

00:32:59.327 --> 00:33:01.837
And it is for those parents as well.

00:33:01.837 --> 00:33:06.247
We wanna get it right and
that's what we're committed to.

00:33:06.247 --> 00:33:09.127
And I don't have a magic wand

00:33:09.127 --> 00:33:13.367
and it's gonna look very, very
different in every community.

00:33:13.367 --> 00:33:16.127
And by the way, there
are some communities,

00:33:16.127 --> 00:33:20.587
this is the most creative
one that's online learning

00:33:20.587 --> 00:33:22.047
that I've heard of, and it's in Maryland.

00:33:22.047 --> 00:33:24.507
It's a state, the state of Maryland.

00:33:24.507 --> 00:33:25.920
They have a plan, whoa.

00:33:26.759 --> 00:33:29.147
So they're the outlier.

00:33:29.147 --> 00:33:31.757
But their plan was, look,

00:33:31.757 --> 00:33:34.247
we probably could open this building.

00:33:34.247 --> 00:33:37.297
It's super-disinfected with all the masks

00:33:37.297 --> 00:33:39.747
and the health screenings,

00:33:39.747 --> 00:33:44.747
but we can put a small
fraction of the kids in here

00:33:44.807 --> 00:33:46.637
and socially distance.

00:33:46.637 --> 00:33:49.068
So they said, so let's go with that.

00:33:49.068 --> 00:33:52.970
We're going to do distance learning.

00:33:54.327 --> 00:33:57.027
We are going to have a
plan for distance learning

00:33:57.027 --> 00:33:59.207
so that it's not just, you know,

00:33:59.207 --> 00:34:01.557
we're closing the school
tomorrow, go do distance learning.

00:34:01.557 --> 00:34:04.027
Everyone has planned for this.

00:34:04.027 --> 00:34:07.467
We're gonna plan on how we're
gonna deliver those lunches

00:34:07.467 --> 00:34:09.007
and those homework packets.

00:34:09.007 --> 00:34:11.227
We're gonna use the
buses in a reverse way.

00:34:11.227 --> 00:34:13.597
They're going to be delivering
things to the kids' homes,

00:34:13.597 --> 00:34:15.827
not picking up the kids
to go to the school.

00:34:15.827 --> 00:34:18.882
And so all hands on deck,
they're all doing it.

00:34:18.882 --> 00:34:21.872
But they said there are some kids

00:34:21.872 --> 00:34:24.430
who might not have mom and dad at home.

00:34:25.547 --> 00:34:27.541
We can hand 'em a laptop.

00:34:27.541 --> 00:34:31.677
We might even be able to set
up some kind of Wi-Fi broadband

00:34:31.677 --> 00:34:36.237
in a parking lot somewhere,
but mom and dad aren't there

00:34:36.237 --> 00:34:40.825
to bring the kids to download
something in a parking lot.

00:34:40.825 --> 00:34:45.507
They're not there to explain
to a second or third grader

00:34:45.507 --> 00:34:47.207
what it is that we're doing.

00:34:47.207 --> 00:34:51.817
So they're going to open up
their schools to those kids

00:34:51.817 --> 00:34:55.697
who need the most, who don't
have it in their homes.

00:34:55.697 --> 00:35:00.177
And they're not giving in-person
instruction to those kids.

00:35:00.177 --> 00:35:04.830
Those kids are opening up the
school laptop and tablets.

00:35:05.832 --> 00:35:10.697
There's adult supervision,
but the teacher is online,

00:35:10.697 --> 00:35:13.987
and it's some of the
paraprofessionals who were saying,

00:35:13.987 --> 00:35:15.257
all right, I can help you.

00:35:15.257 --> 00:35:19.867
Like your mom or dad would be
helping you if they had a job

00:35:19.867 --> 00:35:23.507
where they were working from
home or had access to that.

00:35:23.507 --> 00:35:24.767
And for those teachers

00:35:24.767 --> 00:35:28.177
that are teaching special education kids,

00:35:28.177 --> 00:35:33.177
especially the medically
fragile kids who need so much

00:35:34.177 --> 00:35:38.387
and they're in a very specific atmosphere.

00:35:38.387 --> 00:35:41.407
They're saying we're
gonna bring those kids in.

00:35:41.407 --> 00:35:44.417
There are very few of
them, we can distance,

00:35:44.417 --> 00:35:48.515
we can disinfect, and we're
not opening the school.

00:35:48.515 --> 00:35:49.837
♪ Ta da ♪

00:35:49.837 --> 00:35:54.807
But we're making sure
that everybody has access

00:35:54.807 --> 00:35:59.094
to what the most fortunate
kids have access to.

00:35:59.094 --> 00:36:01.787
So I'm so proud of educators.

00:36:01.787 --> 00:36:03.577
And there are lots of school boards

00:36:03.577 --> 00:36:07.137
that are listening to us,
as they say, all right,

00:36:07.137 --> 00:36:10.877
Dr. Fauci says that it's not
ready to open this school.

00:36:10.877 --> 00:36:13.327
We still want teaching
and learning going on.

00:36:13.327 --> 00:36:18.327
You're the professionals, tell
us how we can do this safely

00:36:18.397 --> 00:36:20.207
and do it competently.

00:36:20.207 --> 00:36:23.207
And it's happening in more
places than you think.

00:36:23.207 --> 00:36:25.798
What makes the news is we did it wrong.

00:36:25.798 --> 00:36:29.707
We opened the school,
hundreds of kids got sick.

00:36:29.707 --> 00:36:30.630
Front page news.

00:36:32.967 --> 00:36:35.917
And it is a cautionary tale,
it should be front page news.

00:36:35.917 --> 00:36:38.897
But those places that have quietly done it

00:36:38.897 --> 00:36:40.787
the way they were supposed to,

00:36:40.787 --> 00:36:42.657
they listened to medical experts,

00:36:42.657 --> 00:36:45.007
they listened to education experts.

00:36:45.007 --> 00:36:47.997
They said, parents, well,
what can you do in your homes?

00:36:47.997 --> 00:36:50.467
What can't you do in your homes?

00:36:50.467 --> 00:36:53.457
Those are the places that
are quietly showing us

00:36:53.457 --> 00:36:54.660
the way forward.

00:36:55.497 --> 00:36:58.527
And maybe we can give
a little more attention

00:36:58.527 --> 00:37:01.407
to the, not just the cautionary tales,

00:37:01.407 --> 00:37:02.790
but the success stories.

00:37:04.027 --> 00:37:04.860
- No, I love that.

00:37:04.860 --> 00:37:05.977
The example you gave of Maryland,

00:37:05.977 --> 00:37:06.810
actually I hadn't heard of it

00:37:06.810 --> 00:37:08.137
and I hadn't heard of that model,

00:37:08.137 --> 00:37:09.887
but it makes a ton of sense.

00:37:09.887 --> 00:37:13.967
And I'm going add that to my
talk track of ideas for folks.

00:37:13.967 --> 00:37:15.087
I also giggled a little bit,

00:37:15.087 --> 00:37:18.047
'cause I also get the question
that you, I guess, got around

00:37:18.047 --> 00:37:21.387
will distance learning replace
teachers and all of that.

00:37:21.387 --> 00:37:23.087
And I almost laugh every time I hear that.

00:37:23.087 --> 00:37:25.537
'Cause I'm sometimes a
bit of a poster child

00:37:25.537 --> 00:37:27.077
for online learning or whatever else

00:37:27.077 --> 00:37:28.557
and I'm the first one to say,

00:37:28.557 --> 00:37:31.257
if I had to pick between a
live teacher and technology,

00:37:31.257 --> 00:37:33.257
I would pick the live
teacher any day for my kids

00:37:33.257 --> 00:37:34.877
or anyone else's kids.

00:37:34.877 --> 00:37:38.257
And pure distance learning
is not a good solution

00:37:38.257 --> 00:37:40.297
for the great majority of students.

00:37:40.297 --> 00:37:42.407
What we should be thinking about is

00:37:42.407 --> 00:37:45.721
how do we get all of the
power of an amazing teacher,

00:37:45.721 --> 00:37:48.587
especially ideally once
we're post-pandemic,

00:37:48.587 --> 00:37:51.157
in person so that you have
that, you're sharing a room,

00:37:51.157 --> 00:37:52.047
you can have that hug,

00:37:52.047 --> 00:37:53.967
you can have that human
to human connection.

00:37:53.967 --> 00:37:56.487
And then think about how
tools can be in service,

00:37:56.487 --> 00:37:58.367
once again, not technology
for technology's sake,

00:37:58.367 --> 00:38:01.487
but technology and service
to true pedagogical

00:38:01.487 --> 00:38:03.307
or social, emotional goals.

00:38:03.307 --> 00:38:04.397
So it couldn't be more aligned.

00:38:04.397 --> 00:38:06.617
And you know I'd love to go offline

00:38:06.617 --> 00:38:08.107
and talk about how we can help.

00:38:08.107 --> 00:38:10.654
'Cause I think we're very aligned on this.

00:38:10.654 --> 00:38:15.654
And just in the remaining time
we have, what do you think,

00:38:16.720 --> 00:38:18.627
everything we're describing,

00:38:18.627 --> 00:38:20.677
this is clearly a suboptimal year.

00:38:20.677 --> 00:38:23.499
Not everyone's going to be
able to do what Maryland's did.

00:38:23.499 --> 00:38:28.137
There's gonna be kids that
are gonna fall further behind,

00:38:28.137 --> 00:38:29.617
not be supported.

00:38:29.617 --> 00:38:31.487
It's probably, this year's
probably gonna drive

00:38:31.487 --> 00:38:33.280
more inequity, not less.

00:38:34.167 --> 00:38:36.057
What do you think's gonna
be the outcome of it?

00:38:36.057 --> 00:38:38.127
If we're talking about, if
we're having this conversation

00:38:38.127 --> 00:38:41.407
in five years and we say,
yeah, COVID changed a lot,

00:38:41.407 --> 00:38:44.267
and this is what the outcome
of it was, positive, negative.

00:38:44.267 --> 00:38:47.341
I'd love to, you know, put
your prognostication hat,

00:38:47.341 --> 00:38:49.777
what are we gonna be
looking at permanently

00:38:49.777 --> 00:38:53.227
in the education system or
in society because of this?

00:38:53.227 --> 00:38:57.057
- I really appreciate the
gravity of that question.

00:38:57.057 --> 00:38:59.807
And I don't think we ask it enough.

00:38:59.807 --> 00:39:02.787
We're gonna look back on this year

00:39:02.787 --> 00:39:05.517
as a lost year for so many kids

00:39:06.407 --> 00:39:11.407
because of the political lack
of will to take this seriously

00:39:15.807 --> 00:39:17.950
and to do what needed to be done.

00:39:19.137 --> 00:39:24.137
The magical thinking of too
many key political figures,

00:39:26.057 --> 00:39:31.057
local, state and national,
has devastated lives.

00:39:33.027 --> 00:39:36.917
And not just the people who
lost someone, lost their health

00:39:36.917 --> 00:39:40.920
or their life to COVID,
but these schoolchildren.

00:39:43.734 --> 00:39:47.177
I just keep saying this
because you can't see it

00:39:47.177 --> 00:39:50.107
because I turned my
computer away from the wall

00:39:50.107 --> 00:39:52.867
that I have punched several holes in.

00:39:52.867 --> 00:39:57.867
Just so upset and so
angry at the poor judgment

00:39:59.437 --> 00:40:03.377
of the magical thinking of politicians

00:40:03.377 --> 00:40:06.287
who got really, really good advice.

00:40:06.287 --> 00:40:07.747
I'm a sixth grade teacher.

00:40:07.747 --> 00:40:09.157
I understood that advice.

00:40:09.157 --> 00:40:12.867
I understood, I'm not a scientist,

00:40:12.867 --> 00:40:16.967
but anybody could explain
or listen to the explanation

00:40:16.967 --> 00:40:18.577
and say, well, yeah, that makes sense.

00:40:18.577 --> 00:40:19.967
And these politicians

00:40:19.967 --> 00:40:21.667
that could have done something about it,

00:40:21.667 --> 00:40:23.897
I hate to just dwell on that.

00:40:23.897 --> 00:40:28.897
But these kids have been
sacrificed to the bad judgment

00:40:32.267 --> 00:40:34.237
of too many politicians.

00:40:34.237 --> 00:40:38.780
And I want to make it my life's mission

00:40:38.780 --> 00:40:43.780
to try and recapture some
of their loss this year.

00:40:47.367 --> 00:40:50.220
And it's gonna be a really tough job.

00:40:51.317 --> 00:40:54.307
What we should be doing going forward

00:40:54.307 --> 00:40:58.427
I think with new and better decisions

00:40:58.427 --> 00:41:01.987
will be what are we going
to go back and take,

00:41:01.987 --> 00:41:06.247
especially those kids that had access

00:41:06.247 --> 00:41:09.727
and opportunity to nothing, if anything.

00:41:09.727 --> 00:41:11.577
I'm not even just talking about,

00:41:11.577 --> 00:41:12.767
well, look at all the folks

00:41:12.767 --> 00:41:16.047
that didn't get to go to
their graduation party.

00:41:16.047 --> 00:41:18.567
Yeah, that's really sad.

00:41:18.567 --> 00:41:21.507
I'm not talking about that.

00:41:21.507 --> 00:41:24.517
I'm talking about someone,
you only get one year

00:41:24.517 --> 00:41:26.427
to be a first grader.

00:41:26.427 --> 00:41:30.267
You only get one year
to put the consonants

00:41:30.267 --> 00:41:32.677
and the vowels together
and have it click and go

00:41:32.677 --> 00:41:36.337
I get it, this is called
reading, oh, my Lord.

00:41:36.337 --> 00:41:39.477
And to have fun so that you make reading

00:41:39.477 --> 00:41:42.710
something that you just
do because you love it.

00:41:43.592 --> 00:41:47.407
For some of these kids,
we're gonna have to go back

00:41:47.407 --> 00:41:52.297
and give them something
extra and give them something

00:41:53.307 --> 00:41:57.207
not just remedial but
something where we say

00:41:57.207 --> 00:41:59.527
you're the kids that
we're not going to put

00:41:59.527 --> 00:42:03.587
in an overcrowded classroom
and expect to get caught up.

00:42:03.587 --> 00:42:07.857
You're the kids that we are
going to prioritize the tools

00:42:07.857 --> 00:42:10.337
and the techniques and the programs

00:42:10.337 --> 00:42:12.707
that we're gonna be doing with you

00:42:12.707 --> 00:42:14.767
that are going to incorporate the arts

00:42:14.767 --> 00:42:16.677
and sports and field trips.

00:42:16.677 --> 00:42:19.037
We are going to do something to bring joy

00:42:19.037 --> 00:42:23.227
into your learning
experience that was lost

00:42:23.227 --> 00:42:25.100
because of this lost year.

00:42:26.047 --> 00:42:30.737
Funding for public schools has
been miserable for so long.

00:42:30.737 --> 00:42:35.657
And I reject people who, well-meaningly,

00:42:35.657 --> 00:42:39.167
they will think they're saying
something real and they say,

00:42:39.167 --> 00:42:44.167
schools are the fundamental
foundation of our economy.

00:42:44.377 --> 00:42:46.527
It's our best investment.

00:42:46.527 --> 00:42:48.707
All right, so yes.

00:42:48.707 --> 00:42:51.897
And for me that's like four on the list.

00:42:51.897 --> 00:42:56.877
It's a human and civil
right to an education.

00:42:56.877 --> 00:43:01.877
I don't care if that choir
puts one dime into the economy.

00:43:02.287 --> 00:43:05.967
It was that child's right
to have that experience.

00:43:05.967 --> 00:43:10.077
It is the foundation of our democracy

00:43:10.077 --> 00:43:13.907
of being a good citizen,
of being a good neighbor,

00:43:13.907 --> 00:43:18.047
being a good parent, a good human being.

00:43:18.047 --> 00:43:23.017
So there's so much wrapped
up in that public school,

00:43:23.017 --> 00:43:24.368
I'm gonna start crying.

00:43:24.368 --> 00:43:26.587
We forget that.

00:43:26.587 --> 00:43:31.587
And we think of it as a
transactional piece of the economy.

00:43:31.857 --> 00:43:33.397
It's so much bigger than that.

00:43:33.397 --> 00:43:38.397
And we have got, we owe,
we owe this lost year

00:43:39.737 --> 00:43:41.737
to millions of kids,

00:43:41.737 --> 00:43:44.350
and we should make that
a national priority.

00:43:45.867 --> 00:43:48.077
- Well, no, I couldn't
agree with you more, Lily.

00:43:48.077 --> 00:43:50.497
And I'm saying this completely genuinely,

00:43:50.497 --> 00:43:53.457
view Khan Academy as in service to you

00:43:53.457 --> 00:43:56.612
and all of the teachers
that you're representing.

00:43:56.612 --> 00:43:59.567
We view ourselves that way,
and especially in this crisis,

00:43:59.567 --> 00:44:03.904
we wanna do whatever we can
to mitigate the suboptimality

00:44:04.965 --> 00:44:07.037
of what we're all facing this coming year,

00:44:07.037 --> 00:44:09.937
and to help mitigate and
close whatever gaps we can

00:44:09.937 --> 00:44:13.977
in the aftermath of
whatever all of this is.

00:44:13.977 --> 00:44:15.737
But really appreciate the conversation.

00:44:15.737 --> 00:44:17.757
I learned a ton, and I hope that this is

00:44:17.757 --> 00:44:20.113
the start of our conversations together.

00:44:20.113 --> 00:44:23.180
- Absolutely, thank you
so much for having me.

00:44:24.327 --> 00:44:25.477
- Thanks so much, Lily.

00:44:27.317 --> 00:44:31.047
Well, thank you all for
joining what I, again,

00:44:31.047 --> 00:44:32.447
a fascinating conversation.

00:44:32.447 --> 00:44:33.957
The whole world is going through

00:44:33.957 --> 00:44:36.667
a very tough set of
circumstances right now,

00:44:36.667 --> 00:44:38.647
but I think the school issue

00:44:38.647 --> 00:44:41.647
and the ability to make sure
that inequity doesn't grow

00:44:41.647 --> 00:44:43.587
as we go through school closures,

00:44:43.587 --> 00:44:45.977
or physical school
closures, as Lily mentioned,

00:44:45.977 --> 00:44:48.357
we're gonna hopefully
continue to educate kids

00:44:48.357 --> 00:44:49.420
in any ways we can.

00:44:50.647 --> 00:44:52.937
I think it's the issue
of our day, you know,

00:44:52.937 --> 00:44:53.897
that and the economics.

00:44:53.897 --> 00:44:56.069
And the education one, in a lot of ways,

00:44:56.069 --> 00:44:58.287
